g (candidate 3)** pays 585 more in fees and saves only about 365 in holding.\n\n**Choosing among the every-2-days plans.** A shortage costs 24 per unit (17 lost revenue plus the 7 penalty). An extra unit bought costs 11 plus holding. Unsold demand is lost, not carried forward, so I modelled stock as a random walk that cannot go below zero. Mean demand is 1.046 × forecast. For product 1 from day 4:\n\n| Candidate | Ordered (mean demand about 1088) | Expected lost units | Product 1 cost (purchase + lost + holding) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 1277 | about 5 | much higher: large surplus and holding |\n| 6 | 1145 | about 24 | about 13 744 |\n| 5 | 1104 (+16 buffer, then forecast demand) | about 34 | about 13 384 |\n| 7 | 1040 (about 3.7 short of mean demand every cycle) | about 75 | about 13 466 |\n\nFor product 0 (mean demand about 290), candidate 7 (277 ordered) comes out about 56 better than candidate 5 (295 ordered). Candidate 6 (305 ordered) is worse than both.\n\n**Overall.** Candidate 5 is ahead of candidate 7 by about 30 out of a total of roughly 17 000, which is within my estimation error. Candidate 6 is about 350 behind, and candidate 2 is far behind. I chose candidate 5 because it is also more robust. It builds a buffer early, while stock is near zero and shortages are most likely, and after that it orders forecast demand. Candidate 7 orders less than mean demand in every cycle, so it is more exposed to demand spikes."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6184, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6184}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:02:57.404Z", "request_id": "req_011CgF2dFqV3aapt