obal", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:03:33.021Z", "request_id": "req_011CgF2nV5xwnreMEVczhLqm", "thinking_duration_ms": 114968}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgF2nVnNyjddmReyLqRPY", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I pick **Candidate 5**. I estimated this in my head on forecast means, without running a simulation, so the figures below are approximate.\n\n**1. Order frequency (fixed order fee plus holding)**\n- **Every 4 days (candidate 4):** only 7 order days, but each delivery has to cover 4 days. That holds more stock. On mean demand the warehouse also overflows: around day 16, roughly 30 units of product 1 are lost on arrival. That costs more than the order fees saved.\n- **Every 3 days (candidates 0 and 1):** 9 order days. Average stock is half a day's demand higher than with 2-day ordering, about 14 per day in holding, or about 350 over the period. The saving on order fees is only 180. Candidate 0 also hits the 200-unit capacity around days 8–10.\n- **Daily (candidate 3):** 26 order days cost 1170 in fees. That is 585 more than 2-day ordering, while holding saves only about 350. On top of that, its quantities run about 10 % above forecast.\n- **Every 2 days (candidates 2, 5, 6, 7):** 13 order days. This is the best trade-off between fees and holding, with no capacity problems.\n\n**2. Safety stock within the 2-day group**\n\nThe plan is fixed in advance and unsold demand is lost, not carried over. So stock behaves like a random walk that cannot go below zero. Product 1 is decisive here. It